## Best Practices

### Writing Effective Instructions

#### 1. Be Specific and Actionable

```markdown
# ❌ Vague
Use good selectors for UI elements

# ✅ Specific
Use test IDs as first choice for selectors:
- Preferred: [data-testid="submit-button"]
- Acceptable: button:has-text("Submit")
- Avoid: .btn.btn-primary (generic classes)
```

#### 2. Provide Context

```markdown
# ❌ Without Context
Don't use page.$()

# ✅ With Context
Don't use page.$() or page.$$() - these are deprecated Playwright methods.
Instead, use page.locator() which provides:
- Auto-waiting capabilities
- Better error messages
- Retry mechanisms
- Type safety with TypeScript
```

#### 3. Use Real Examples

```markdown
# ❌ Generic Example
Create a step definition for clicking

# ✅ Real Example from Codebase
When("User click on {string} on {string} page", async ({ page, locators }, locatorName, pageName) => {
    let selector = locators.getLocatorFromPageByName(pageName, locatorName);
    await page.locator(selector).click();
});
```

#### 4. Explain the "Why"

```markdown
# ❌ Command Without Reason
Always use ConfigUtil.getUrl() for URLs

# ✅ With Rationale
Always use ConfigUtil.getUrl() for URLs instead of hardcoding.
Why: Environment-specific URLs change across test/staging/production.
ConfigUtil reads from autotests.environments.json based on TARGET_ENV,
enabling the same tests to run across all environments.
```

#### 5. Prioritize DO's Over DON'Ts

```markdown
# ⚠️ Focuses on negatives
Don't hardcode URLs
Don't use implicit waits
Don't skip error handling

# ✅ Focuses on positives
Use ConfigUtil.getUrl() for all URLs
Rely on Playwright's auto-waiting (no explicit waits needed)
Add try-catch blocks with meaningful error messages
```

### Structuring for Copilot

#### Use Markdown Hierarchy

Copilot better understands well-structured documents:

```markdown
# Main Topic (H1) - Domain name
## Category (H2) - Major sections
### Subsection (H3) - Specific topics
#### Detail (H4) - Fine-grained points
```

#### Use Lists for Scanability

```markdown
# ✅ Easy to scan
Available step definitions:
- `User click on "element" on "page" page` - Click interaction
- `User fill in text "value" in "field" on "page" page` - Text input
- `Element "element" should be visible on "page" page` - Visibility check

# ❌ Hard to scan
There are several step definitions available including clicking on elements,
filling in text fields, and checking visibility of elements on pages.
```

#### Use Code Fences with Language Tags

```markdown
# ✅ Syntax highlighted and clear
```typescript
const response = await apiClient.get("APIUrl", "/api/users");
expect(response.status()).toBe(200);
```

# ❌ Unclear and unstyled
const response = await apiClient.get("APIUrl", "/api/users");
expect(response.status()).toBe(200);
```

#### Use Tables for Comparisons

```markdown
| Selector Type | Priority | Example | When to Use |
|---------------|----------|---------|-------------|
| Test ID | 1 (Highest) | `[data-testid="login"]` | Always prefer |
| Accessible | 2 | `button:has-text("Submit")` | When test IDs unavailable |
| CSS | 3 | `.login-button` | Stable classes only |
| XPath | 4 (Last resort) | `//button[@id="submit"]` | Complex DOM navigation |
```

### Keeping Instructions Concise

#### Balance Detail with Brevity

```markdown
# ❌ Too brief
Use fixtures for API calls.

# ❌ Too verbose
The framework implements a fixture-based dependency injection pattern
utilizing Playwright's test.extend() API to provide shared resources
across test scenarios. For API testing, the apiClient fixture is
instantiated as an instance of ApiClientBase class which wraps...

# ✅ Just right
Use the `apiClient` fixture for API requests. It's available in all
step definitions and wraps Playwright's APIRequestContext:

```gherkin
Given User make GET request to "APIUrl" with path "/api/users"
```

The fixture handles URL resolution via ConfigUtil automatically.
```

#### Link Instead of Repeating

```markdown
# ❌ Duplicates content
API Testing uses the ApiClientBase class which has get(), post(),
and put() methods... [repeats full API documentation]

# ✅ Links to canonical source
API Testing uses the ApiClientBase class. See the class documentation
in `core/api/api.client.base.ts` for available methods.

For step definitions using apiClient, see:
- `.github/instructions/api-testing.instructions.md`
```

